--[[
The always-available intrinsic standard-library namespace.

`nupp` is a table the prelude declares, so its fields are in scope everywhere
without a `require`; each implementation loads only if the checked program
actually uses it. A binding called `nupp` shadows it deliberately, the way a
binding called `string` would.

```nupp
local text = nupp.data.json.encode({ok = true})
nupp.log.info("wrote %d bytes", #text)
```

This is compiler-provided language surface, not an ambient source module. This
file supplies its generated API overview; it is not loaded as the runtime
implementation. Declaring `module nupp` here would turn the intrinsic root into
an ordinary module and make `nupp.data`, `nupp.io`, and the other intrinsic
members ambiguous with real descendants.

Its public namespaces are [](nupp.data) (JSON, UTF-8, UUIDs, hashes and
checksums), [](nupp.io) (buffers, byte views, readers, writers, and the typed
scalar cursors over them), [](nupp.io.path) and [](nupp.io.uri) (filesystem
paths and resource identifiers), [](nupp.math) (scalar helpers and
two-dimensional vectors), `nupp.peg` (textual parsing-expression grammars
compiled at either phase), and `nupp.reflect` (semantic type descriptors and
reflection-driven keyed codecs).

Borrowing operations also live under this global: `nupp.borrow`,
`nupp.borrowFrom`, `nupp.region`, and `nupp.pin`. `nupp.region` is the unsafe
audited bridge from runtime-validated bounds to a checked child interval.
Ownership itself is expressed by built-in compile-time type-generator calls such
as `affine(T, cleanup)`, `affine(T)`, and `pinned(T)`, while `drop`, `unsafe
adopt`, and `unsafe release` are language operations over affine values.

The source files shipped beside it are declared modules, reached with `require`
or qualified module paths and separate from the intrinsic table:

```nupp
local zone = require("nupp.profile.zone")
```

Those are [](nupp.profile) and the [](nupp.profile.zone) markers it reads, the
[](nupp.mem) storage family ([](nupp.mem.span), [](nupp.mem.heap),
[](nupp.mem.soa), and the [](nupp.mem.indexed) range they share), the
[](nupp.owners) dynamic-owner custody containers, and `nupp.compiler`, which holds the self-hosted compiler
implementation and is hidden from public API documentation by its `@!internal`
namespace root.

See [standard-library.md](docs/concepts/standard-library.md) for what belongs here and what does not,
and [modules.md](docs/concepts/modules.md) for how a declared module is reached.
]]

const nupp = {}
return nupp
